  "source_text": "Ines watched him do it. Later, she would tell Nadia: He didn't ask permission. He just walked there. Like the room had rearranged itself around him.\n\n\"We go forward,\" Adrian said.\n\nHe let the words settle. Nobody spoke. Sophie poured wine.\n\n\"The zero-day hit thirteen hours ago,\" Adrian said. \"Their recovery estimate is Tuesday noon. We aim to trigger before then. Any delay in their patching helps us; any improvement costs us. We can't plan on an extension.\"\n\n\"Thirty-five hours for a plan that was designed to take weeks,\" Ines said flatly.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"With no digital infrastructure.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"No AI coordination, no encrypted communications, no automated deployment.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nInes looked at Sophie. \"Is there enough wine for this?\"\n\n\"There is not,\" Sophie said, and poured herself a glass anyway.\n\n\"Here's what we have,\" Adrian said. He put Seb's final notes on the table. \"Ines. The Scaffold survived?\"\n\nInes nodded. \"Thousands of nodes across forty-seven countries. Peer-to-peer. No single point of failure. I designed it to survive exactly this scenario, the scenario where everything else goes wrong. The nodes are sleeping. I wake them up, they begin replicating. Once data hits the network, it's like trying to un-ring a bell.\"\n\n\"Andrei. The zero-days.\"\n\nAndrei spoke without looking up from the spot on the table he'd been staring at since he arrived. \"Each exploit is custom. Six target companies, six different security architectures, six keys. They're stored on encrypted drives in a safe house in Tallinn. Backups exist. The exploits are intact.\" He paused. \"But they must work on the first attempt. Each company's security team is in crisis mode right now, they're patching everything. Every hour we wait, the probability of success decreases.\"\n\n\"Tomás. The devices.\"\n\n\"Six devices in Lisbon,\" Tomás said. \"A courier is retrieving them while we talk. I meet him on the way west. Then three plants in the US. The other three go to operatives who already have access.\"\n\n\"Sophie.\"\n\n\"The financial forensics tools are ready,\" Sophie said. \"Offshore accounts, shell companies, PAC donations, dark money flows. I've been mapping the connections between the council members and every lawmaker they've purchased for the past two years. The tools go active the moment we're ready. The money trails are the evidence that turns a data dump into a criminal case.\"\n\n\"Ólafur.\"\n\nThe young man looked up. \"The trigger has to make every implant activate within four hundred milliseconds of the others. The fastest inter-company warning takes five seconds. We need to be inside that interval. My Iceland setup is compromised. I have the synchronization logic, but I need to recalibrate it on the London hardware.\"\n\n\"How long?\"\n\n\"Once I reach London? Twenty hours. Eighteen if the hardware is what I requested.\"\n\nThe journey would consume part of that margin. The rest belonged to faults they hadn't found yet.\n\nAdrian looked around the room.\n\n\"We leave within the hour,\" Adrian said. \"Reserve identities. Separate routes. Retrieve what's been staged, verify it, and prepare for deployment. Nadia handles the messages. Don't use the compromised channels.\"\n\n\"And if it doesn't work?\" Andrei asked.\n\n\"Then everything Seb built, everything Priya died for, everything we've sacrificed, it all vanishes. The oligarchs patch their systems, they hunt down what's left of the movement, and in six months no one will remember any of this happened. The window closes, and it doesn't open again.\"\n\nThe silence that followed was the loudest sound Adrian had ever heard.\n\nIt was Sophie who broke it. She set down her wine glass with the precision of a woman placing a chess piece.\n\n\"I didn't spend two years mapping the financial anatomy of six oligarchic empires to stop now because we lost our fancy computer,\" she said. \"I have the receipts. I intend to deliver them.\"\n\nInes almost smiled. \"The Scaffold is my best work. I built it to survive the end of the world. I'd like to turn it on.\"\n\nTomás said nothing. He simply zipped his bag and stood.\n\nAndrei met Adrian's eyes for the first time. \"The exploits are ready. They've been ready. It's what happens after that I'm afraid of.\"",
